C = [
("h1", "11. Titus 2:13: “The Great God and Our Saviour”"),
("p", "Claim: Titus 2:13 is best read as naming one person, “the great God and our Saviour Jesus Christ,” and the other punctuation is possible. The volume argues the first reading from the sentence and from Paul’s habit of calling both God and Christ “Saviour” in the same letter. It does not say which coming “the glorious appearing” names."),
("sub", "11.1 The setting"),
("p", "The verse closes a short passage on grace. “For the grace of God that bringeth salvation hath appeared to all men, Teaching us that, denying ungodliness and worldly lusts, we should live soberly, righteously, and godly, in this present world” (Titus 2:11, 12). Then: “Looking for that blessed hope, and the glorious appearing of the great God and our Saviour Jesus Christ; Who gave himself for us, that he might redeem us from all iniquity, and purify unto himself a peculiar people, zealous of good works” (Titus 2:13, 14)."),
("sub", "11.2 One person or two"),
("p", "The question is whether “the great God” and “our Saviour Jesus Christ” are two persons or one. The King James Version leaves both readings open in English. The volume argues for one, for four reasons. First, in the Greek the two titles stand under one article, which the English cannot show. Second, “the glorious appearing” is a single appearing, and the sentence says “appearing of the great God and our Saviour,” and not of two. Third, Paul in this letter calls both the Father and Christ “Saviour”: “God our Saviour” and “the Lord Jesus Christ our Saviour” in one greeting (Titus 1:3, 4), and again “God our Saviour” and “Jesus Christ our Saviour” in one sentence (Titus 3:4, 6). The word is used of both, and so a sentence that gives the title “Saviour” to Christ in the same breath with “God” is in the letter’s manner. Fourth, the next verse, “Who gave himself for us,” has a single subject, and the one who gave himself is Jesus Christ."),
("p", "Thomas Constable reads it the same way: “Paul described the appearing and glory of one Person: our great God and Savior, Christ Jesus.” He adds that “This is another of the passages that clearly states that Jesus Christ is God.” The volume cites both statements as readings of the sentence and agrees with them."),
("sub", "11.3 The other reading"),
("p", "The other reading takes “the great God” as the Father and “our Saviour Jesus Christ” as the Son, and reads the appearing as the Son’s appearing in the glory of the Father. It is a possible reading of the English, and some translators prefer it. The volume notes it, and notes that on either reading Titus 2:13 is a statement of the highest possible dignity for Christ, who is the Saviour whose appearing the church awaits. The thesis does not rest on this verse alone, and it would stand if the other reading were preferred."),
("sub", "11.4 “The glorious appearing”"),
("p", "The verse does not say which appearing is meant. Paul uses the word of the Cross-and-gospel coming of the Saviour in 2 Timothy 1:10, “the appearing of our Saviour Jesus Christ, who hath abolished death,” and of the day to come in 1 Timothy 6:14, “until the appearing of our Lord Jesus Christ.” In Titus 2:13 the words are “Looking for that blessed hope, and the glorious appearing.” Constable names the event he takes it to be. The volume reports that, and does not say which coming it is. That question belongs to the volume on the resurrection order."),
("ctbl", "“Saviour” in the letter to Titus.", [
    ("The verse", "Who is called Saviour", "What it leaves unsaid"),
    ("Titus 1:3", "“God our Saviour”", "Whether God here is the Father only"),
    ("Titus 1:4", "“the Lord Jesus Christ our Saviour”", "How one Saviour is named of both"),
    ("Titus 2:13", "“the great God and our Saviour Jesus Christ”", "Whether the title is one person’s or two persons’; see 11.2 and 11.3"),
    ("Titus 3:4", "“God our Saviour”", "The same question as 1:3"),
    ("Titus 3:6", "“Jesus Christ our Saviour”", "How the same title is used of both"),
  ], [0.18, 0.48, 0.34]),
("sub", "11.5 The other passage on the Saviour’s appearing"),
("p", "The same letter describes the appearing in the past tense in its third chapter. “But after that the kindness and love of God our Saviour toward man appeared, Not by works of righteousness which we have done, but according to his mercy he saved us, by the washing of regeneration, and renewing of the Holy Ghost; Which he shed on us abundantly through Jesus Christ our Saviour” (Titus 3:4–6). The subject is “God our Saviour,” and the means is “Jesus Christ our Saviour.” The saving is attributed to God and to Christ in a single sentence, with the Holy Ghost as the one shed. The passage is cited here for the pattern, not for a doctrine of the Spirit. The pattern is that Paul writes of God and of Jesus Christ as Saviour, and of what each did, without setting them in competition."),
("sub", "11.6 What the volume takes from the chapter"),
("bul", [
    "The best reading of Titus 2:13 is one person, “the great God and our Saviour Jesus Christ,” and the other reading is possible.",
    "The letter calls both God and Jesus Christ “Saviour,” which fits the first reading.",
    "The sentence goes on, “Who gave himself for us,” with one subject.",
    "The volume does not say which appearing is meant, and rests nothing on this verse alone.",
  ]),
("p", "The sayings now collected name Christ as the form of God, the image, the Creator, the one in whom the fulness dwells, God manifest in the flesh, and the great God and Saviour. The next chapter turns to the other side of Paul’s writing: the man."),
]
